data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing delivers the Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA into a cell, cutting the genome at a chosen site so genes can be removed or new ones added [1].\n- It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences derive from phage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phage DNA in later infections [2].\n- Gene editing makes targeted insertions, deletions, or substitutions in DNA, and CRISPR is one example [3].\n- Reported uses include a 2021 story on a CRISPR breakthrough for treating diseases [4] and a 2024 post on a study reporting HIV eliminated in cell culture with CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

